so ukraine was able to force thousands  of russian troops out of the kharkiv  region in a matter of days but along the  border with russia the shelling  continues unabated  dw's nick connolly has been to meet  people rebuilding their lives to the  sound of artillery fire  less than a week ago this was a journey  best made in a tank  now just an hour's drive separates  with its restaurants and metro from  villages just emerging from six months  of occupation  here within sight of the border the  outnumbered russian army held on the  longest throwing everything it had at  advancing ukrainian forces most the  locals didn't stick around leaving pets  and livestock behind  sidlana is one of just a handful of  residents left on her street which has  been without power and without phone  signal for the past six months and as of  last week without a roof  i came out of the cellar and after the  bang it seemed like everything was all  right i had to go down again and when i  left everything was gone the door the  window and the roof all just gone  the blast wave took it straight off all  that's left are my skis  and it wasn't the first time after two  previous hits sit lana was able to patch  things up but not this time  many of her neighbours had it worse  a boy next door was killed and two  houses down the road there was a  neighbor a veteran of the soviet army  he was sitting in his yard and out of  nowhere a piece of shrapnel hit him in  the forehead and killed him  the russians kept asking me why i didn't  leave i turned it around and asked them  why they came in the first place i've  been here for 39 years  a light sleeper svetlana tells us she  hopes she'll wake up in time the ceiling  does end up falling down  like millions of other ukrainian  refugees svetlana's daughter and  grandson are now in poland  with her family scattered she relies on  her neighbor michaela for help  but six months of occupation have taken  their toll on him as a man of fighting  age michalo was constantly in the sights  of the russian army  i only left the house during the day  you just have to keep your head down  the first time the russians picked me up  was in april  a few times a month they would get me in  again  i still have the marks from the bullets  they shot at my legs  they were obsessed with finding out  where my brother was in the military and  where his documents were hidden  michalo says his father was taken away  by the russians in march since then all  trace of him has been lost on both sides  of the border  this is just some of the shrapnels that  lana has collected in her living room  just in the space of the last few days  with no end in sight to shelling along  the border why not take this chance to  get out  she tells us ukrainian troops offered to  take her to kharkiv in their tank her  response was simple  not until i've repaired my roof  for more analysis of the situation on  the ground we are now joined by marina  myron she is an honorary research fellow  in the defense studies department center  for military ethics at king's college in  london thank you so much for joining us  on the program  now we've seen  impressive gains by the ukrainian army  in the last few days can we expect  something similar in the near future  that is correct we have seen some really  impressive offensive capabilities in the  north so in the region of issum which is  the  park oblast  close to russia's border  however we're still seeing some  difficulties in the south so essentially  the reason for the success um it was  a surprise to the russian forces as they  have moved their troops to the south  expecting the uh so called her son  offensive however they couldn't hold the  ground in the north and the amount of  ukrainian troops deployed for the  northern offensive was outnumbering  whatever was left there of the russian  forces so the best they could do  was retreat to save manpower  right now the question is that was a  tactical game for the ukrainian military  however they need to hold that ground  and so it is too early to tell how that  will turn out  that being said they definitely  undermined russia's plans in in military  tactical sense now it's interesting that  you would say that especially the last  bit because the russian president  vladimir putin just said that the  counter-offensive that came as a  surprise as you say won't change  russia's plans that russia has so far  not deployed its full forces and he  added quote we aren't in a rush  what do you make of that  well obviously it was a slap for russia  in a tactical sense and the whole idea  was to be able to surround slovanska and  chromatorsk and repeat the lysid chance  scenario now on the ground they will  have to regroup and change their plan  and they're seeing obviously the  continuous push in the curson region and  that is strategically  much more important um obviously there  needs to be kind of some diplomatic exit  for putin in order to keep the morale  high of his forces on the ground  and of course they are trying to  mobilize more forces right now as we  speak however occur son is difficult in  terms of logistical support to the  troops so it remains to be seen how that  plays out  so marina just briefly if you would  looking at  the other side the west  can ukraine  handle this offensive or the  counter-offensive on its own does it  need more support from the west and will  it get it  that's a great question because um  obviously the counter offensive took  place now six months into the war the  ukrainian forces have received an  enormous amount of help from the west  and both in military equipment as well  as in training  yet we have to think about the seasons  and about the fact that the winter is  approaching and about ukrainian economy  which  is  about to crumble according to the world  bank they need  349 billion  for recovery of the ukrainian economy so  on the one hand they have to sustain  that war effort on the other hand they  will have to provide for the population  and we have seen that russia has been  targeting critical infrastructure and  we've seen blackouts throughout the  country so that makes things more  complicated and we have a bigger picture  with you know the russian gas and oil  supplies in europe so um that money has  to come from somewhere as a help that  ukraine needs and in how far european  countries  are willing to sacrifice the good of  their own citizens to help ukraine  remains to be seen that's that's the big  question indeed uh marina myron an  honorary research fellow in the defense  studies department center for military  ethics at king's college in london thank  you so much  thank you
 

